course: dictionary
lesson: 5924
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The verb marry is a good example. «A parent's new partner is not a new mum or dad; if they marry or live together, they become a step-parent.» Here marry means to become someone's husband or wife in a wedding.
explain: The word can also mean to perform the wedding itself. «The priest married them.» Now the priest is the one doing the marrying. Read the words around marry to see which meaning fits.
explain: Marry can also mean to join or combine two things well, though this meaning is less common. When a word has several meanings, try each one in the sentence and keep the one that makes sense.
fact: Marry is a verb, so its forms are marry, marries, married and marrying.
fact: «Being engaged means two people have promised to marry each other, and a ring is often given as a sign of the promise.»
fact: Get married means to become husband and wife in a wedding.
fact: Marry into a family means to become part of that family by marrying someone in it.
exercise: «Being engaged means two people have promised to marry each other.» What does marry mean here? | *To become each other's husband or wife | To eat a big meal together | To perform a wedding for two friends
exercise: «The priest married them.» What does married mean here? | The priest ran away from them | The priest gave them a present | *The priest performed the wedding | The priest tidied their house
exercise: Ellie says marry means to cook a meal. What does marry really mean? | To go to sleep | *To become someone's husband or wife in a wedding | To sing a song | To make a sandwich
test: «When Odysseus got home, many men were feasting in his hall, wanting to marry his wife Penelope.» What does marry mean here? | To be very happy | *To become Penelope's husband | To drive guests out of a hall | To eat a big meal together
test: Which phrase means that two people have promised to marry? | Get married | *Engaged | Marry into | Married to
exam: Which meaning fits a sentence like «The cook's sauce and the gravy marry well together»? | To cook a meal | *To join or combine two things well | To become husband or wife | To perform a wedding
